TITLE:: PltHistogram
summary:: Histogram of a collection of values
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltView, Classes/PlotLib

DESCRIPTION::
Counts of a collection over equal bins. Useful for looking at what a distribution
actually produces, which is often not what its name suggests.

The counting is link::Classes/PlotLib#*histogram::, so a distribution can be
checked without opening a window.

A non-empty bin is drawn at least one pixel tall, so rare values stay visible.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: values
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
Range to bin across. Values outside it are counted into the end bins.
argument:: bins
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: fromCounts
Plot counts that were binned elsewhere.
argument:: counts
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: data_
Rebin a new collection over the same range and refresh.

METHOD:: normalized
The proportion of the total in each bin, which is the shape a density is judged by.

METHOD:: counts
METHOD:: lo
METHOD:: hi
METHOD:: bins
METHOD:: barColor
METHOD:: alpha
METHOD:: label
Appended to the caption.

EXAMPLES::
code::
PltHistogram({ 1.0.rand } ! 8000, 0, 1, 40, "Pwhite").front;
PltHistogram({ exprand(0.01, 1.0) } ! 8000, 0, 1, 40, "Pexprand").front;

// the mean of three uniforms has a standard deviation of a third of the sum's,
// which a histogram shows at a glance
PltHistogram({ ({ 1.0.rand } ! 3).sum / 3 } ! 8000, 0, 1, 40, "mean of three").front;

// counts from somewhere else
PltHistogram.fromCounts([3, 9, 14, 8, 2], 0, 5, "given counts").front;
::
